(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-metabolism-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Quiz 3 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Vitamin A is a lipid-soluble vitamin found only in animal products, though carotenes in plants serve as provitamin A. It is stored in the liver for approximately how long, and is needed for visual pigments, epithelial cell growth/maintenance, and reproduction?","choices":["It is not stored at all, being fully water-soluble","5–10 days","5–10 hours","5–10 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin A is stored in the liver for roughly 5–10 months, a substantial reserve consistent with its being a lipid-soluble (rather than water-soluble) vitamin, and it is required for visual pigments, epithelial cell growth/maintenance, and reproduction. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 19"},{"q":"Thiamine (Vitamin B1) is required for decarboxylation of pyruvate and of intermediaries in the citric acid (Krebs) cycle. Without adequate thiamine, the body must draw...","choices":["more energy from fat and ketone bodies","more energy from anaerobic glycolysis alone, with no other changes","no alternative energy source, since carbohydrate oxidation continues unaffected","exclusively on protein breakdown for energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because thiamine is needed for the decarboxylation steps that let pyruvate and citric acid cycle intermediates be oxidized for energy, thiamine deficiency forces the body to rely more heavily on fat and ketone bodies for energy instead. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 20"},{"q":"Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) serves as a hydrogen carrier in oxidation reactions. Flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD), derived from riboflavin, functions in the citric acid cycle by...","choices":["synthesizing coenzyme A from pantothenic acid","converting oxaloacetate directly to glucose","decarboxylating pyruvate directly in the mitochondria","oxidizing succinate to fumarate, becoming FADH2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FAD, a riboflavin-derived electron/hydrogen carrier, oxidizes succinate to fumarate within the citric acid cycle, becoming FADH2 in the process and contributing electrons to the electron transport chain. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 21"},{"q":"Niacin (Vitamin B3) functions as a component of nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD), essential for oxidation reactions, and can also be synthesized endogenously from...","choices":["the amino acid glutamate exclusively","cholesterol","the amino acid tryptophan","glucose directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unlike most vitamins, niacin can be synthesized in the body from the amino acid tryptophan, supplementing dietary intake, in addition to its essential role as a component of NAD in oxidation reactions. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 22"},{"q":"Pantothenic acid (Vitamin B5) is required for fat and carbohydrate metabolism chiefly because it is a structural part of...","choices":["collagen protein","myelin sheath","hemoglobin protein","coenzyme A (CoA)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pantothenic acid is a structural component of coenzyme A, the essential cofactor used throughout fat and carbohydrate metabolism (e.g., forming acetyl-CoA), which is why pantothenic acid is required for these pathways. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 23"},{"q":"Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) supports carbohydrate metabolism as part of the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase, whose job is to...","choices":["release glucose-1-phosphate from glycogen","synthesize glycogen from amino acids","add glucose units onto a growing glycogen chain","convert glucose-6-phosphate to glucose-1-phosphate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Glycogen phosphorylase, which requires pyridoxine (B6) as a cofactor, catalyzes the release of glucose-1-phosphate from stored glycogen — the first step of glycogenolysis — and pyridoxine also serves as a coenzyme in amino acid transamination. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 24"},{"q":"Vitamin E, a lipid-soluble vitamin found in vegetable oils and fresh greens, mainly functions in the body as a(n)...","choices":["coenzyme in amino acid transamination","structural component of coenzyme A","essential cofactor for blood clotting","antioxidant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin E's main physiological function is as an antioxidant, helping protect cell membranes and other structures from oxidative damage. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 29"},{"q":"Hypothalamic appetite regulation involves two opposing neuron populations: POMC/CART neurons, which suppress appetite, and which opposing population that stimulates appetite?","choices":["NPY/AGRP neurons","leptin/ghrelin neurons","POMC/CART neurons themselves, acting in both directions","insulin/glucagon neurons"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. POMC/CART neurons in the hypothalamus suppress appetite (are anorexigenic), while NPY/AGRP neurons have the opposite, appetite-stimulating (orexigenic) effect — together forming a key hypothalamic circuit for long-term energy balance regulation. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 12"},{"q":"In a lean 70-kg man, liver triglyceride stores represent a strikingly small energy reserve — about 450 kcal, or roughly what fraction of total body energy stores?","choices":["About 0.3%","About 30%","About 3%","Nearly 100%, since the liver is the main energy storage organ"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Despite the liver's central metabolic role, its triglyceride store (about 450 kcal) makes up only around 0.3% of total body energy reserves in a lean 70-kg man — dwarfed by adipose tissue's much larger fat stores. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 3"},{"q":"In a typical American diet, the approximate percentage breakdown of daily energy intake by macronutrient is...","choices":["about 45% carbohydrate, 40% fat, and 15% protein","about 90% fat, 5% carbohydrate, and 5% protein","about 33% each of carbohydrate, fat, and protein","about 80% protein, 10% fat, and 10% carbohydrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A typical American diet derives roughly 45% of energy from carbohydrate, about 40% from fat, and about 15% from protein — even though fat provides more than twice the energy per gram (about 9 kcal/g) compared with carbohydrate or protein (about 4 kcal/g each). — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 4"},{"q":"In the oxidative deamination example where alanine is first transaminated to pyruvate (producing glutamate), the subsequent breakdown of that glutamate (glutamate + NAD+ + H2O) yields NADH, H+, ammonia, and...","choices":["pyruvate molecule","alpha-ketoglutarate","acetyl-CoA unit","oxaloacetate ion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oxidative deamination of glutamate (glutamate + NAD+ + H2O → NADH + H+ + NH3 + alpha-ketoglutarate) removes the amino group as ammonia while regenerating alpha-ketoglutarate, a citric acid cycle intermediate that can be used for energy or further metabolism. — Chapter 70 (Protein Metabolism), Slide 14"},{"q":"The hypothalamic preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus contains both heat-sensitive and cold-sensitive neurons, while skin and deep body temperature receptors mainly detect...","choices":["only warm and hot temperatures, functioning to prevent hyperthermia","no temperature information at all, relying entirely on the hypothalamus","cold and cool temperatures, functioning to prevent hypothermia","blood glucose level rather than temperature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral (skin) and deep body temperature receptors are mainly tuned to detect cold and cool temperatures, serving primarily to help prevent hypothermia, while the hypothalamic preoptic area itself contains both heat- and cold-sensitive neurons for central temperature sensing. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 5"},{"q":"The posterior hypothalamus, which receives input from the anterior hypothalamus and peripheral receptors, mainly serves to...","choices":["regulate hunger and satiety exclusively, playing absolutely no thermoregulatory role","detect temperature directly on its own, with no input from any other brain region","control heart rate exclusively, entirely unrelated to body temperature regulation","integrate temperature signals and coordinate the appropriate heat-conserving or heat-losing responses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The posterior hypothalamus integrates temperature information relayed from the anterior hypothalamus (central heat/cold-sensitive neurons) and peripheral receptors, then coordinates the body's overall response — triggering heat-conserving mechanisms (like vasoconstriction, shivering) or heat-losing mechanisms as needed. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 5"},{"q":"The skin's effectiveness as a heat 'radiator' varies enormously with blood flow: heat conduction through the skin differs by approximately what factor between maximal vasodilation and maximal vasoconstriction?","choices":["About 1.5-fold","There is no meaningful difference regardless of vasodilation/constriction","About 50-fold","About 8-fold"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Skin blood flow can vary so widely under sympathetic control that heat conduction through the skin differs by roughly an 8-fold factor between maximal vasodilation (high flow, high heat loss) and maximal vasoconstriction (low flow, heat conserved). — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 12"},{"q":"Chylomicrons, after delivering most of their triglyceride to peripheral tissues via lipoprotein lipase, leave behind chylomicron remnants that are then removed from circulation by the...","choices":["liver","spleen","lungs","kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once lipoprotein lipase has stripped most of the triglyceride from a chylomicron, the resulting cholesterol-rich remnant is taken up and cleared from the circulation by the liver. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 9"},{"q":"Very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL), produced by the liver, is distinguished from chylomicrons in that VLDL primarily carries triglyceride synthesized...","choices":["endogenously by the liver itself, rather than from a recent meal","only from dietary cholesterol, never triglyceride","directly from the intestinal lumen without any hepatic processing","exclusively from a meal just eaten, identical to chylomicrons"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. While chylomicrons carry dietary (exogenous) triglyceride absorbed from a meal, VLDL instead carries triglyceride that the liver has synthesized endogenously — for instance, from excess dietary carbohydrate — for export to peripheral tissues. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 24"},{"q":"The liver's contribution to fat digestion, distinct from its many metabolic functions, is its production of...","choices":["gastric hydrochloric acid secretion","bile, needed to emulsify dietary fat","gastric intrinsic factor protein","exocrine pancreatic lipase enzyme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. While most of the liver's roles in lipid metabolism concern synthesis, storage, and lipoprotein handling, its direct contribution to fat digestion itself is producing bile, whose bile salts emulsify dietary fat so pancreatic lipase can act on it efficiently. — Chapter 71 (The Liver), Slide 17"},{"q":"Respiratory quotient (RQ), defined as the ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed, differs depending on which fuel is being oxidized. Pure fat oxidation produces an RQ closest to...","choices":["0.7","2.0","1.0","0.1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fat oxidation yields an RQ of about 0.7, lower than carbohydrate oxidation's RQ of about 1.0, because fat molecules contain relatively less oxygen per carbon and thus require proportionally more O2 consumption relative to CO2 produced. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 6"},{"q":"Assessing protein metabolism (protein catabolism specifically) can be estimated clinically by measuring...","choices":["urinary glucose excretion","exhaled carbon dioxide alone","urinary nitrogen excretion","fecal fat content"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because protein is the body's main nitrogen-containing macronutrient, measuring urinary nitrogen excretion provides an estimate of the rate of protein breakdown (catabolism) occurring in the body. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 7"},{"q":"Interconversions among glucose, fructose, and galactose occur mainly in the liver, allowing the body to convert dietary fructose and galactose into...","choices":["amino acids directly for protein synthesis","glucose, for use in glycolysis or storage as glycogen","vitamin precursors exclusively for coenzyme synthesis","fatty acids exclusively, bypassing glucose entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The liver interconverts fructose and galactose into glucose (or glucose-related intermediates), allowing these dietary monosaccharides to be funneled into the same central pathways — glycolysis or glycogen storage — used for glucose. — Chapter 68 (Carbohydrate Metabolism), Slide 5"},{"q":"Glycogenesis (glycogen synthesis) and glycogenolysis (glycogen breakdown) are regulated in opposite directions by insulin and glucagon, such that epinephrine and glucagon both tend to...","choices":["only affect glycogen metabolism in skeletal muscle, never the liver","stimulate glycogenolysis, raising blood glucose","have no effect on glycogen metabolism","stimulate glycogenesis, lowering blood glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both epinephrine and glucagon act to stimulate glycogenolysis (breakdown of stored glycogen to glucose), raising blood glucose — epinephrine especially during acute stress/exercise, and glucagon especially between meals — opposing insulin's glycogenesis-promoting, glucose-lowering effect. — Chapter 68 (Carbohydrate Metabolism), Slide 11"},{"q":"Triglycerides such as tristearin are formed by esterifying glycerol with three fatty acid chains. In the body, the synthesis of new triglyceride from glucose therefore requires glucose-derived...","choices":["glycerol (via glycolysis) plus fatty acids (via lipogenesis)","neither glycerol nor fatty acids, since triglycerides cannot be synthesized from glucose","only fatty acids, since glycerol comes from dietary fat exclusively","only glycerol, since fatty acids come from dietary protein exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Building a new triglyceride requires both a glycerol backbone — supplied via glycolysis — and fatty acid chains, which can themselves be synthesized from excess glucose-derived acetyl-CoA through lipogenesis, then esterified together to form the finished triglyceride. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 18"},{"q":"ATP generated by cellular metabolism is used by the body for several major categories of work, most notably...","choices":["only passive diffusion processes occurring across the plasma membrane","only DNA replication occurring during the S phase of the cell division cycle","muscle contraction, active transport across membranes, and synthesis of chemical compounds","only muscle contraction, with no other significant cellular use at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ATP's energy is consumed by the body across three broad categories of cellular work: mechanical work (muscle contraction), active transport of substances against a concentration or electrical gradient, and synthesis of new chemical compounds (biosynthesis). — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 2"},{"q":"During brief, strenuous bursts of activity, muscle can generate ATP anaerobically using stored phosphocreatine and glycogen (via glycolysis to lactate) before aerobic metabolism ramps up. This anaerobic capacity is especially important because...","choices":["it requires no ATP regeneration mechanism of any kind whatsoever during exercise","it is the only energy source the body ever uses, even during complete rest","it produces substantially more total ATP per glucose molecule than aerobic metabolism does","it allows near-immediate energy production without waiting for increased oxygen delivery to catch up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anaerobic energy production (from phosphocreatine breakdown and glycolysis to lactate) can begin almost instantly, supplying ATP during the initial seconds of intense activity before cardiovascular and respiratory adjustments can sufficiently increase oxygen delivery to the working muscle. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 3"},{"q":"The liver protects the body from many ingested and internally produced chemicals through a detoxification process that typically involves...","choices":["converting the toxic substance directly into a functional plasma clotting factor protein","physically trapping the substance permanently within hepatocytes with no chemical change","chemically altering (e.g., via conjugation or oxidation) the substance to make it less toxic and easier to excrete","excreting the substance completely unchanged into the bile on every single occasion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic detoxification generally works by chemically modifying a potentially harmful substance — through processes like conjugation or oxidation — converting it into a less toxic, more water-soluble form that can then be excreted in bile or urine. — Chapter 71 (The Liver), Slide 9"},{"q":"Failure of the liver to adequately clear ammonia (generated from amino acid deamination) is dangerous chiefly because ammonia...","choices":["directly lowers blood glucose to dangerous levels","is toxic to the central nervous system","directly causes excessive blood clotting","is entirely harmless and requires no clearance mechanism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ammonia is neurotoxic; if the liver cannot convert it into urea for safe excretion, rising ammonia levels can impair central nervous system function, which is why efficient hepatic ammonia clearance via the urea cycle is so important. — Chapter 71 (The Liver), Slide 9"},{"q":"Sympathetic nervous system activation can raise metabolic rate in part by...","choices":["releasing norepinephrine/epinephrine, which stimulate cellular metabolism and can promote thermogenesis","directly inhibiting thyroid gland hormone secretion entirely and permanently","reducing both heart rate and overall cardiac output substantially below resting levels","having no measurable effect whatsoever on overall resting metabolic rate at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic activation releases norepinephrine and epinephrine, which stimulate cellular metabolic activity broadly and can promote thermogenesis (heat-generating metabolism), contributing to a higher overall metabolic rate. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 9"},{"q":"Ketone bodies are produced by the liver mainly during states of low insulin/high glucagon (such as prolonged fasting), when fatty acid breakdown generates more acetyl-CoA than the...","choices":["skeletal muscle tissue is able to store away as new glycogen","kidneys can filter, requiring hepatic conversion to a filterable form","citric acid cycle can immediately process, given limited oxaloacetate availability","pancreas is able to convert directly into additional circulating insulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. During prolonged fasting, oxaloacetate is diverted toward gluconeogenesis, leaving less available to combine with the large amount of acetyl-CoA generated from accelerated fatty acid breakdown; the liver instead condenses the excess acetyl-CoA into ketone bodies, which peripheral tissues (including, eventually, the brain) can use as an alternative fuel. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 16"},{"q":"During prolonged starvation, once fat stores become the dominant fuel source, ketone bodies become especially important because they...","choices":["cannot be used as fuel by any tissue at all, and simply accumulate as metabolic waste","are produced exclusively within skeletal muscle tissue, never in the liver","directly and completely replace the need for oxygen in cellular respiration","can cross the blood-brain barrier and serve as an alternative fuel for the brain, sparing protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike most fatty acids, ketone bodies can cross the blood-brain barrier and be used as an alternative fuel by the brain during prolonged starvation, reducing the brain's dependence on glucose and thereby sparing body protein that would otherwise need to be broken down for gluconeogenesis. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 8"},{"q":"Amino acids that are broken down into acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA (rather than glucose precursors) are classified as 'ketogenic' because their carbon skeletons can be used to form...","choices":["new glucose exclusively, via gluconeogenesis","new essential amino acids exclusively","ketone bodies or fatty acids, but not new glucose","urea directly, bypassing the citric acid cycle entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ketogenic amino acids feed into acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA, the same intermediates used to build ketone bodies or fatty acids — but because this entry point cannot be reversed into net new glucose synthesis, ketogenic amino acids cannot contribute to gluconeogenesis the way glucogenic amino acids can. — Chapter 70 (Protein Metabolism), Slide 17"}]};
